# Python security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security guidance to Python's own
OWASP-style sinks and the safe API each one has a direct replacement for.
Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files (W1-AC8).

## Command and code execution

- Never call `subprocess.run`/`Popen`/`call` with `shell=True` or a single
  interpolated command string; pass an argument list
  (`subprocess.run(["cmd", arg])`) so the shell never re-parses untrusted
  input.
- Never pass untrusted input to `eval`/`exec`, `compile()` of untrusted
  source, or `os.system` — there is no safe way to sandbox these; parse the
  input with a real parser (`json`, `ast.literal_eval` for literals) instead.
- Treat `ast.literal_eval` as the only acceptable "eval-like" call on
  untrusted text, and only for literal Python values, never arbitrary
  expressions.

## Deserialization

- Never unpickle (`pickle.load`/`loads`, `dill`, `shelve`) data from a
  source you do not fully trust and control — unpickling untrusted bytes is
  equivalent to `exec`. Use `json` or a schema-validated format instead.
- Use `yaml.safe_load`, never `yaml.load`, for any YAML that did not
  originate from your own trusted config in the repo.
- Parse XML with `defusedxml` (or `ET.parse` with entity resolution
  disabled) instead of raw `xml.etree.ElementTree`/`xml.dom.minidom` on
  untrusted input — the stdlib parsers resolve external entities (XXE) by
  default.

## Data access

- Build SQL with parameterized queries (`cursor.execute("... WHERE id = %s",
  (id,))` or the ORM's own query builder), never by interpolating values
  into a SQL string with f-strings/`%`/`.format()`.
- Validate and normalize any filesystem path built from user input before
  using it; resolve with `Path(...).resolve()` and check it stays under the
  intended base directory to prevent path traversal (`../../etc/passwd`).

## Secrets and randomness

- Generate tokens, session IDs, and password-reset codes with the `secrets`
  module (`secrets.token_urlsafe`, `secrets.compare_digest`), never `random`
  — `random` is not cryptographically secure and its output is predictable.
- Never hard-code a credential, API key, or secret in source; read it from
  the project's configured secret store or environment, and never log it.

## Network calls

- Always pass an explicit `timeout` to `requests`/`httpx` calls — an
  unbounded call to a slow or hung endpoint can hang the whole process.
- Never set `verify=False` (requests) or disable TLS certificate validation
  to work around a cert error; fix the underlying trust-store/cert issue
  instead.

## Dependencies

- Run the project's dependency audit tool (`pip-audit`, or `uv pip list
  --outdated` plus the project's configured scanner) before adding a new
  third-party dependency with security-sensitive functionality (auth,
  crypto, deserialization, subprocess wrapping).
- Pin dependencies the project already pins (lockfile present) rather than
  loosening a version constraint to resolve a conflict without checking the
  changelog for the versions in between.

## Red flags

- "I'll just use `shell=True` here, the input is only ever a filename" — an
  attacker-controlled filename can still contain shell metacharacters;
  always pass an argument list.
- "`eval` is fine, this string only comes from our own config file" — if the
  config file is ever user-editable or fetched remotely, this stops being
  true; prefer `ast.literal_eval` or `json.loads` regardless.
